Subject: main: read the 32-bit environ through a 64-bit copy on OpenVMS

PHP declares "extern char **environ" and walks it for $_ENV, $_SERVER,
getenv() and phpinfo().  With clang's 64-bit pointers on OpenVMS that is
wrong twice over: the C RTL's environ is a 32-bit pointer to an array of
32-bit pointers (vms-php probe r_environ), so PHP read a pointer with junk
in its upper half and PHP.EXE ACCVIOed before running any script.  On VMS,
environ becomes a call returning a 64-bit, NULL-terminated copy.
---
--- a/main/php.h
+++ b/main/php.h
@@ -262,7 +262,15 @@
 /* global variables */
 #ifndef PHP_WIN32
 #define php_sleep sleep
+#ifdef __VMS
+/* The C RTL's environ is a 32-bit pointer to an array of 32-bit pointers,
+ * which 64-bit code cannot walk as char **: read it through a 64-bit copy
+ * (vmsport/vms_shims.c in vms-php). */
+char **vms_php_environ(void);
+#define environ vms_php_environ()
+#else
 extern char **environ;
+#endif
 #endif	/* ifndef PHP_WIN32 */
 
 extern const char php_build_date[];
